    "body": "© Rehman Asad\nBy Bangladesh correspondent\n2 November 2026\nBangladesh’s garment shippers face missed sailings and mounting pressure on delivery schedules as labour protests halt production and disrupt cargo movements to Chittagong port.\nProduction was suspended at around 690 garment factories today, according to the latest information from Bangladesh, while roadblocks hampered the movement of export cargo from Dhaka factories to the port.\nThe disruption threatens shipment schedules on two fronts: factories may struggle to complete orders on time; and finished garments face delays reaching their booked vessels.\nFor forwarders, prolonged unrest could mean rearranging collections and bookings as cargo readiness dates slip. Shipments missing sailing cut-offs could face longer delays, while buyers seeking to protect delivery deadlines may have to consider more-expensive airfreight.\nHowever, there was no confirmed estimate of cargo delays, missed sailings, or additional demand for air transport as yet.\nWorkers continued demonstrations on Monday in Dhaka’s Mirpur area, Ashulia, Gazipur, and Tongi, demanding a new minimum wage and improved working conditions. Police and Border Guard Bangladesh personnel were deployed, with clashes reported with protesters.\nFactory owners have threatened indefinite closures if the protests continue, raising the prospect of further disruption to production and exports.\nFazlee Shamim Ehsan, president of the Bangladesh Knitwear Manufacturers and Exporters Association, said the unrest was already affecting overseas trade, and added: “Moreover, the ongoing labour movement is hampering production and exports. Buyers are worried.”\nHe warned that the disruption coincided with uncertainty already present in Bangladesh’s largest apparel market.\n“Our largest market, Europe, is going through severe instability. As a result, European buyers will obviously purchase less now then in the past.”\nGazipur Industrial Police superintendent Amjad Hossain said more than 150 factories in his area had closed today because of the protests, and the nationwide closure figure rose during the day, the latest tally putting suspended production at around 690 factories. At least 500 factories had closed on Sunday amid fears of vandalism.\nThe government had formed a new wage board yesterday, headed by a district judge and included representatives of factory owners and workers, to determine a new minimum wage.\nWorkers are seeking a minimum monthly wage of Tk30,000, approximately $250. Their other demands include food rationing in garment factories, implementation of trade union rights, a judicial investigation into workers’ deaths, and an end to unjustified lay-offs.\nThe formation of the board brought no immediate end to the demonstrations, with roadblocks and factory closures forming.\nFor garment buyers and their logistics providers, the immediate uncertainty is when factories can resume production and whether completed orders can reach the port in time.\nAny extended shutdown would leave less room to recover lost production and transport time before contractual delivery deadlines.\nCheck out today’s podcasts: News in Brief, focusing on ocean freight with Xeneta’s Peter Sand ; and a bonus episode: news from TIACA ‘s Air Cargo Forum in Miami.",
